From: Liminix AX6600 bring-up
Subject: ath11k: pci: safe remove when firmware never registered (SBL-stuck bind)

A late sysfs bind can return 0 (probe completes: CE pipes allocated, MHI
powered) while the firmware never actually boots -- it stalls in the MHI
SBL handshake and no QMI FW_READY/FW_INIT_DONE event ever arrives.  In
that state ATH11K_FLAG_QMI_FAIL is NOT set (no QMI event failed; the
events just never came) and ATH11K_FLAG_REGISTERED is unset: the device
is bound with no netdev.

Unbinding such a device (e.g. our ath11k-late retry, or manual
unbind+bind) used to take the normal ath11k_core_deinit() path whose
core_stop() -> ath11k_pcic_stop() -> ath11k_ce_cleanup_pipes() reads the
CE srng head/tail registers of a firmware that never brought the rings
up; the mappings are gone and the read faults. Observed on JDCloud
AX6600 (QCN9074 on PCIe, IPQ6010):

  Internal error: Oops: 96000005 (translation fault)
  pc: ath11k_hal_srng_access_begin+0xc
  ... ath11k_ce_cleanup_pipes -> ath11k_pcic_stop ->
      ath11k_core_stop -> ath11k_core_deinit ->
      ath11k_pci_remove -> (sysfs unbind)

Treat a bound-but-never-registered device exactly like the QMI_FAIL
case: power the device down and tear down without ever running
core_deinit()/CE cleanup.  A fully working card has ATH11K_FLAG_REGISTERED
set at remove time (it is only cleared much later during mac80211
teardown inside core_deinit), so normal and post-RDDM removes are
unaffected.

Signed-off-by: Liminix AX6600 bring-up <liminix@invalid>
---
diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/pci.c b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/pci.c
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/pci.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/pci.c
@@ -1198,7 +1198,16 @@
 
 	ath11k_pci_set_irq_affinity_hint(ab_pci, NULL);
 
-	if (test_bit(ATH11K_FLAG_QMI_FAIL, &ab->dev_flags)) {
+	/* Liminix: a late sysfs bind can return success while the firmware
+	 * never boots (stuck in the MHI SBL handshake, no QMI FW_READY).
+	 * ATH11K_FLAG_QMI_FAIL is NOT set then (no QMI event failed, none
+	 * arrived), but ATH11K_FLAG_REGISTERED is unset and the CE rings
+	 * were never brought up, so core_deinit() faults reading srng
+	 * registers.  Treat bound-but-not-registered like QMI_FAIL:
+	 * teardown without touching CE.  See commit message for the trace.
+	 */
+	if (test_bit(ATH11K_FLAG_QMI_FAIL, &ab->dev_flags) ||
+	    !test_bit(ATH11K_FLAG_REGISTERED, &ab->dev_flags)) {
 		ath11k_pci_power_down(ab, false);
 		ath11k_debugfs_soc_destroy(ab);
 		ath11k_qmi_deinit_service(ab);
 		ath11k_core_pm_notifier_unregister(ab);
 		goto qmi_fail;
 	}
 
 	set_bit(ATH11K_FLAG_UNREGISTERING, &ab->dev_flags);
